with q as (
  select $astroOcean_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter opens by tallying worlds with liquid water today. What is the count it arrives at, and what does that count argue?", "options": ["Roughly equal numbers inside and outside the habitable zone, showing that distance from the Sun does not matter much either way.", "The inner, sunlit solar system has exactly one world with liquid water (Earth), while the frozen outer dark has at least five confident cases (Europa, Ganymede, Callisto, Enceladus, Titan) and closer to eight or nine counting strong candidates like Triton, Pluto, and Mimas.", "The inner solar system has three ocean worlds (Earth, Venus, Mars) versus only Europa and Enceladus outside, so the classical picture broadly holds.", "Only Europa and Enceladus have confirmed oceans, making outer-system oceans a rare curiosity rather than the rule."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The count is the argument. In the warm, sunlit inner solar system exactly one world has surface liquid water: Earth. In the frozen outer dark the confident cases number at least five (Europa, Ganymede, Callisto, Enceladus, and Titan's subsurface water ocean), and counting strong candidates such as Triton, Pluto, and the 'stealth ocean world' Mimas the total reaches eight or nine. So counted by number of worlds holding liquid water, the outer solar system beats the inner and it is not close. The common ingredient across every one of those outer worlds is not sunlight but ice-covered water kept liquid by internal heat, above all the tidal grip of an enormous parent planet. The intuition that habitability follows the Sun's warmth was built on a sample size of one planet; widen the sample and it collapses. That reversal is the thesis the whole chapter is built to earn."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "According to the chapter, what keeps these outer-moon oceans from freezing solid despite surfaces colder than 100 kelvin?", "options": ["A combination of three things: tidal heating (a heat source that needs no star, kept running by orbital resonances), an insulating ice lid that traps the heat, and antifreeze chemistry (salts and ammonia) that lowers the freezing point.", "Residual heat left over from the moons' formation four billion years ago, which has simply not yet leaked away.", "Faint but sufficient sunlight focused and trapped by the moons' thick atmospheres, warming the ice from above.", "Radioactive decay in the rocky cores alone, which supplies all the power needed independent of any tidal effect."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The chapter answers the obvious objection with three ingredients working together. First, the heat source is tidal heating: a moon on an eccentric orbit is flexed as it is squeezed harder near its planet and less when far, dissipating energy like a bent paperclip, and orbital resonances (Io-Europa-Ganymede in a 1:2:4 Laplace chain, Enceladus 2:1 with Dione) keep pumping the eccentricity so the flexing never stops. This is gravitational energy extracted from the orbit, not the star; Europa's tidal power is about 10^11 to 10^12 W and Enceladus radiates 5 to 15 GW from its south pole. Second, the ice shell is a poor conductor and acts as an insulating lid, throttling heat loss so the ocean loses heat only as fast as tides supply it. Third, salts and especially ammonia depress the freezing point, a water-ammonia mix staying liquid down to about 176 K. Formation heat and radiogenic heat are mentioned but tidal heat is 'above all' the reason; sunlight is explicitly ruled out."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How did Galileo's magnetometer establish that Europa has a global salt-water ocean without ever landing?", "options": ["It measured the moon's own permanent magnetic field, whose strength revealed a molten iron core stirring an ocean above it.", "It detected radio emissions from lightning in Europa's atmosphere that could only occur over open liquid water.", "Because Jupiter's tilted, spinning field makes the ambient field oscillate in Europa's frame (about an 11.2-hour synodic period), a conducting salt-water layer produces an induced dipole field that always opposes the change in the driving field and tracks its phase, a signature only a global salty liquid layer can make.", "It found gravitational anomalies as the spacecraft flew past, showing a low-density layer consistent with liquid water beneath the ice."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "This is the first of the chapter's two remote-detection methods and rests on Faraday's and Lenz's laws. Jupiter's magnetic dipole is tilted about 10 degrees to its spin axis and Jupiter rotates in under 10 hours, so a point at Europa's orbit feels a field that sweeps up and down, oscillating with a dominant synodic period of about 11.2 hours. A changing field through a conductor drives currents (Faraday), and those currents flow so their own field opposes the change (Lenz). Europa's salty ocean is a global conducting shell, so it generates a secondary induced dipole that points opposite to the changing part of the ambient field, oscillates at the same period, and, crucially, tracks the phase of the external driving field rather than sitting fixed in the moon's frame. Solid ice and rock do not conduct and cannot do this; a permanent magnet or internal dynamo would be fixed in the moon's frame. The amplitude constrains conductivity times thickness (saltiness and depth of ocean) and the phase lag constrains the depth and geometry. The same experiment gave oceans at Callisto and Ganymede."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Cassini flew repeatedly through the Enceladus plume and tasted its contents. Which finding, and what it establishes, is stated correctly?", "options": ["Free molecular oxygen in the plume, establishing that photosynthesis is occurring in the sunlit upper layers of the ocean.", "Silica nanoparticles of a specific size, establishing hydrothermal vents where water reacts with rock above 90 degrees Celsius on the seafloor.", "Fossilized microbial cells embedded in the ice grains, establishing that Enceladus is inhabited.", "Liquid methane and ethane droplets, establishing that the plume is fed by hydrocarbon lakes like those on Titan's surface."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cassini's dust analyser found silica nanoparticles of a very specific size. Silica dissolves into water only under particular conditions and precipitates as nanograins when hot, mineral-laden water meets cold water, which on Earth means hydrothermal vents; the grain size points to water reacting with rock above 90 degrees Celsius at the ocean floor. That is one item on a menu that also included sodium salts (proving a genuine liquid salty sea in contact with rock), molecular hydrogen plus carbon dioxide (a redox chemical battery of the exact kind that powers methanogens), complex organics (the carbon feedstock of biochemistry), and phosphates at concentrations higher than Earth's oceans (closing the last gap, since phosphorus is the scarcest element life requires). The wrong options invert the story: there is no oxygen-photosynthesis finding, no cells were detected (Cassini carried no life-detection instruments), and the methane and ethane are Titan's surface lakes, not Enceladus's plume. Every ingredient life needs was found, but ingredients are not organisms."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does Europa Clipper orbit Jupiter and perform about 49 fast flybys of Europa rather than settling into orbit around Europa itself?", "options": ["Because Europa's weak gravity cannot hold a spacecraft in a stable orbit, so repeated flybys are the only way to stay near it.", "Because flybys are cheaper and use less fuel than orbit insertion, a compromise forced by the mission's budget.", "Because Europa has no solid surface features worth mapping from a fixed orbit, so a survey of many different regions by flyby is scientifically preferable.", "Because Europa orbits deep inside Jupiter's radiation belts, where unshielded electronics survive only hours to days; orbiting Jupiter on a long ellipse keeps the craft mostly in mild radiation far out, dipping in only briefly for each flyby so the total dose stays survivable for years."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The obstacle is radiation. Jupiter's magnetosphere traps and accelerates electrons and ions to lethal energies, filling the inner Jovian system with belts that would destroy unshielded electronics in hours to days, and Europa orbits inside those belts. A spacecraft in a low circular orbit around Europa would sit in that hard radiation continuously and be killed within weeks, before any sustained science. So Europa Clipper (launched October 2024, arriving 2030) orbits Jupiter on a long looping ellipse that spends most of its time far out where radiation is mild, dipping in for a fast flyby of Europa, about 49 of them, each lasting only hours before the craft climbs back to safety and its electronics recover. The looping trajectory is not a budget compromise but the only way to study Europa closely. This is also the chapter's stated irony: the same tilted, spinning Jovian field that reveals the ocean by inducing a detectable field is the field whose trapped particles forbid orbiting the moon. JUICE can orbit Ganymede because Ganymede sits farther out, beyond the worst belts."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Cassini found that Enceladus is habitable but could not determine whether it is inhabited. Explain precisely what that distinction means, why the five plume findings establish only the first and not the second, and describe a measurement that would actually distinguish an inhabited ocean from a merely habitable one and why Cassini could not make it.", "rubric": "A strong answer draws the sharpest line in astrobiology: habitable is a statement about conditions (life could survive there), while inhabited means life actually is present. It should note that the five findings (salt water = a genuine liquid ocean in contact with rock; silica nanoparticles = hydrothermal vents with water-rock chemistry above 90 C; molecular hydrogen with CO2 = a redox chemical energy source, the couple methanogens exploit; complex organics = carbon feedstock; phosphates above Earth-ocean concentration = the scarcest essential element, closing the gap) together demonstrate that every known requirement for life is present: liquid water, an energy source, all necessary elements (C, H, O, N, P, S), and organic raw material. The key argument: none of these five is a product of biology specifically; salts, silica, hydrogen, organics and phosphates all arise from ordinary abiotic water-rock chemistry. A fully stocked warm kitchen is not proof anyone is cooking; a jar of ingredients is not a cake. To show inhabitance one needs a signature abiotic chemistry does not readily counterfeit: strong isotopic fractionation (life prefers lighter carbon, biasing 13C/12C), lopsided chirality (life uses one handedness of amino acids where abiotic synthesis makes both equally), a spiky/selective molecular pattern (metabolism produces non-smooth distributions of chain lengths), or a directly imaged cell. Cassini could make none of these: it carried no life-detection package; its mass spectrometer was built to sniff Titan's atmosphere and its dust analyser to study rings, neither designed for isotope ratios, chirality, or microscopy at the required sensitivity. A complete answer proposes a dedicated plume-sampler with a high-resolution mass spectrometer, a chirality analyser, and a microscope, flying the jets slowly to preserve fragile molecules, to answer not what is in the water but whether anything is alive in it."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter ends on the reframing that 'the habitable zone is not where the water is.' State the three assumptions built into the classical habitable-zone definition, show how the outer ocean worlds violate each, and explain what this reversal implies for where the most common habitable environment in the galaxy may be found (including the case of a starless rogue giant).", "rubric": "A strong answer identifies the classical habitable zone as the band of orbital distances where a rocky planet with the right atmosphere can hold liquid water on its surface warmed by starlight (for the Sun, roughly 0.95 to 1.4-1.7 AU on the conservative estimate, with Venus scorched just inside and Mars frozen just outside). It then names the three built-in assumptions and shows each fails for ocean moons: (1) the water sits on a SURFACE, but ocean-moon water is sealed under tens of kilometres of ice; (2) the warmth comes from a STAR, but the moons' heat is tidal, extracted from the orbit by the parent planet via resonance, owing nothing to sunlight (Europa's 10^11-10^12 W dwarfs any solar contribution to its interior); (3) an ATMOSPHERE traps that warmth, but the moons need none because the insulating ice lid does the job instead. Europa orbits at 5.2 AU (about 3x beyond the HZ outer edge) and Enceladus/Titan at 9.5 AU (6x beyond), yet hold most of the solar system's liquid water. The reversal: habitability is a set of CONDITIONS, not a zone in space around a star. The answer should extend this outward: giant planets are common around other stars (thousands known), giant planets acquire their own moon systems (a massive planet captures a sub-disk), orbital resonances arise generically from migration and tidal evolution, and resonances drive tidal heating, so tidally heated ocean moons should be widespread standard equipment, not rare accidents. The strongest answers note the rogue-giant case: a starless giant ejected from its system still tidally heats its moons, so an ice-sealed ocean persists with no star at all, severing habitability from stellar distance entirely and dissolving the 'zone' concept. Conclusion: the most common habitable environment in the galaxy may be a cold ocean moon under ice, far outside any habitable zone, and our four-century search for warmth-and-sunlight was looking in the wrong place with the wrong idea. Credit may also go to noting this makes such worlds nearly invisible to atmospheric spectroscopy of exoplanets."}]$astroOcean_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/ocean-worlds', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
